WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.489 --> 00:00:05.019
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:05.019 --> 00:00:10.490
In an earlier episode, we showcased a technique
for summarizing images not in a word, but

00:00:10.490 --> 00:00:15.910
an entire sentence that actually makes sense.
If you were spellbound by those results, you'll

00:00:15.910 --> 00:00:21.920
be out of your mind when you hear this one:
let's turn it around, and ask the neural network

00:00:21.920 --> 00:00:28.720
to have a sentence as an input, and ask it
to generate images according to it. Not fetching

00:00:28.720 --> 00:00:34.360
already existing images from somewhere, generating
new images according to these sentences. Create

00:00:34.360 --> 00:00:36.480
new images according to sentences. Is this
for real?

00:00:36.480 --> 00:00:42.120
This is an idea, that is completely out of
this world. A few years ago, if someone proposed

00:00:42.120 --> 00:00:47.480
such an idea and hoped that any useful result
can come out of this, that person would have

00:00:47.480 --> 00:00:49.890
immediately been transported to an asylum.

00:00:49.890 --> 00:00:56.110
An important keyword here is "zero shot" recognition.
Before we go to the zero part, let's talk

00:00:56.110 --> 00:01:01.480
about one shot learning. One shot learning
means a class of techniques that can learn

00:01:01.480 --> 00:01:07.670
something from one, or at most a handful of
examples. Deep neural networks typically require

00:01:07.670 --> 00:01:13.579
to see hundreds of thousands of mugs before
they can learn the concept of a mug. However,

00:01:13.579 --> 00:01:19.479
if I show one mug to any of you Fellow Scholars,
you will, of course, immediately get the concept

00:01:19.479 --> 00:01:24.479
of a mug. At this point, it is amazing what
these deep neural networks can do, but with

00:01:24.479 --> 00:01:30.549
the current progress in this area, I am convinced
that in a few years, feeding millions of examples

00:01:30.549 --> 00:01:36.100
to a deep neural network to learn such a simple
concept will be considered a crime.

00:01:36.100 --> 00:01:42.030
Onto zero shot recognition! The zero shot
is pretty simple - it means zero training

00:01:42.030 --> 00:01:48.280
samples. But this sounds preposterous! What
it actually means is that we can train our

00:01:48.280 --> 00:01:55.539
network to recognize birds, tiny things, what
the concept of blue is, what a crown is, but

00:01:55.539 --> 00:02:00.319
then we ask it to show us an image of "a tiny
bird with a blue crown".

00:02:00.319 --> 00:02:05.450
Essentially, the neural network learns to
combine these concepts together and generate

00:02:05.450 --> 00:02:08.540
new images leaning on these learned concepts.

00:02:08.540 --> 00:02:14.530
I think this paper is a wonderful testament
as to why Two Minute Papers is such a strident

00:02:14.530 --> 00:02:20.420
advocate of deep learning and why more people
should know about these extraordinary works.

00:02:20.420 --> 00:02:25.050
About the paper - it is really well written,
there are quite a few treats in there for

00:02:25.050 --> 00:02:31.030
scientists: game theory and minimax optimization,
among other things. Cupcakes for my brain.

00:02:31.030 --> 00:02:35.700
We will definitely talk about these topics
in later Two Minute Papers episodes, stay

00:02:35.700 --> 00:02:40.450
tuned! But for now, you shouldn't only read
the paper - you should devour it.

00:02:40.450 --> 00:02:45.860
And before we go, let's address the elephant
in the room: the output images are tiny because

00:02:45.860 --> 00:02:51.350
this technique is very expensive to compute.
Prediction: two papers down the line, it will

00:02:51.350 --> 00:02:57.030
be done in a matter of seconds, two even more
papers down the line, it will do animations

00:02:57.030 --> 00:03:03.080
in full HD. Until then, I'll sit here stunned
by the results, and just frown and wonder.

00:03:03.080 --> 00:03:07.320
Thanks for watching, and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

